+### Canonical Horizon terrain path (2026-11-07)
+
+- The opt-in `wiiu/worldgen` bridge uses Horizon's original `Assets`, terrain-only asset reload, `RenderAssets`, registry, biome tables, `horizon-world` generator and `horizon-terrain-mesh::Mesher`. Borrowed chunk/section views expose the existing region state and quart-biome arrays. The bridge has one canonical production mesher and no block-edit remeshing or duplicate face/material implementation.
+- GX2 queries the generated atlas dimensions and copies rows using the destination pitch. Its one-edge-padded sprite cells and clamped UVs match Horizon's terrain sampling path. Temporary full-sky lighting remains; translucent meshes and emissive rendering are not implemented.
+- The terrain-only asset loader lazily resolves blockstate-referenced models through Horizon's resolver, model baker and sprite pipeline. It preserves connected-texture and Fusion data when present, while skipping item-model library loading, standalone/gallery models, LabPBR maps and emissive metadata that this diagnostic renderer cannot draw. Desktop `Assets::reload` remains unchanged. The prepared-cache fast path is gated by the pinned preparation stamp/schema and cache-ready marker; `atlas_builder` invalidates the cache before rebuilding.
+- Earlier host checks passed for the canonical mesher, Cafe atomic compatibility and terrain-only assets. The current `world_probe` now verifies runtime generation, canonical mesh vertex validity, atlas contents/dimensions and stage timings only; it no longer tests gameplay or persistence. Its post-cleanup resource-backed probe passed with nine chunks and 18,432 vertices.
+- Cemu 2.6 loaded prior Cafe RPXs and logged `Run title`. One full desktop asset-reload build remained in `Assets::reload` for 10m26s at about 581 MiB; the terrain-only path later completed base sprites then stayed in block-model baking for five minutes at about 599 MiB. A full Overworld build also remained in chunk generation after roughly 50 seconds, with a prior radius-one attempt exceeding five minutes. Cemu remained responsive in the block-bake run and exited cleanly. None of these runs reached terrain-ready or visually verified a frame; no hardware run has been made.
+- The shared `StateModels` catalog resolves each distinct model identifier once while preserving sorted per-transform bake calls. That optimization removed 5,281 repeated resolutions in the prepared 26.3 model set and passed its equivalence fixture and Cafe target check, but a subsequent Cemu RPX still remained in block-model baking for five minutes.
+- This increment removes the standalone block raycast, AABB/player physics, break/place calls, and `wiiu-edits` NBT/region sidecar. The region generator/mesher/GX2 path remains a renderer diagnostic, not a playable Horizon client. Shared `Game` event dispatch now consumes platform-neutral events, while Winit still owns the outer application lifecycle and its renderer remains WGPU.
+
+## Architecture decisions
+
+- Keep Horizon's source and gameplay/data crates identifiable and isolate Wii U code under `wiiu/`.
+- Upstream updateability is a primary constraint. The current branch has not been proven to be a minimal patch: relative to pinned Horizon `69fab597a6`, `git diff --find-renames=50% --stat -- crates` reports 241 changed crate files, including 47 detected renames. Source moves preserve behavior but can make upstream updates harder. Do not extract another subsystem merely because it might later be useful; first demonstrate a concrete Cafe build/runtime blocker, prefer a narrow platform seam and document why the change is needed. Preserve the existing upstream implementation and paths. The intended future patching application should pin and verify its upstream revision, apply reviewed changes with conflict detection, and never overwrite the user's source silently; it has not been implemented.
+- A direct `cargo +nightly check -p horizon-client --no-default-features --target powerpc-nintendo-cafe`, using the Cafe compiler and `--cfg getrandom_backend="custom"`, stops at getrandom 0.2 because its `custom` feature is absent from the client dependency graph. A disposable wrapper in `$scratch\horizonwiiu-original-client-check`, based on the root lockfile and depending on the existing `wiiu/entropy` adapter as well as the real client, enables that feature and reaches the unsupported desktop `arboard` clipboard dependency. It does not complete compilation or link the adapter. Full logs are `$scratch\horizonwiiu-client-cafe-check-20261107.log` and `$scratch\horizonwiiu-client-cafe-adapter-check-20261107.log`. The client manifests still enable desktop input and require Winit/WGPU. Next, design their Cafe boundaries around the real client and demonstrate the next build step; do not replace the clipboard with an unreported stub.
+- Cafe excludes `arboard` in `crates/horizon-client-input/Cargo.toml`; `src/clipboard.rs` retains the existing in-process clipboard behind the same `SystemClipboard` interface. This small upstream edit is required because the real client enables desktop input transitively and the unsupported dependency cannot be removed by an external wrapper. The root workspace lint configuration registers the Cafe target OS for host cfg checks. On WSL in `$workspace/HorizonWiiU`, with `CARGO_TARGET_DIR=$scratch/horizonwiiu-cit-work CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0`, `cargo +nightly test -p horizon-client-input --lib clipboard::tests --jobs 8` passed 7 tests and `cargo +nightly test -p horizon-client-input --lib --no-default-features in_process_system_clipboard_preserves_copied_text --jobs 8` passed. With `RUSTC=$workspace/HorizonWiiU-toolchain/rust-wiiu-cafe/build/host/stage1/bin/rustc`, `cargo +nightly check -p horizon-client-input --no-default-features --target powerpc-nintendo-cafe --jobs 8` passed. The full-client wrapper's Cafe dependency tree no longer contains `arboard` even with desktop input selected. The full desktop-enabled Cafe client still cannot compile Winit, so this is not a linked or runtime verification. These localized target gates should be straightforward to reapply upstream.
+- Cafe excludes mimalloc in `crates/horizon-threads/Cargo.toml`, and the allocator boundary in `src/lib.rs` uses Cafe's system allocator. `src/heap_census.rs` keeps the original allocation tagging and counters around that allocator when census is enabled. `crates/horizon-client/src/game/memory.rs` reports the unavailable mimalloc commit metrics as null on Cafe. These source changes are required because several client dependencies enable mimalloc transitively, which an external wrapper cannot disable. Desktop uses its original mimalloc paths; the target gates are localized for upstream reapplication. On WSL in the repository with `CARGO_TARGET_DIR=$scratch/horizonwiiu-cit-work`, `cargo +nightly test -p horizon-threads --lib heap_census::tests --jobs 16` passed 2/2, `cargo +nightly check -p horizon-client-input --jobs 16` passed after the shared cfg registration, and `CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0 cargo +nightly check -p horizon-client --no-default-features --jobs 16` passed with existing renderer warnings. With the Cafe stage1 `RUSTC`, `cargo +nightly check -p horizon-threads --features alloc-census --target powerpc-nintendo-cafe --jobs 16` passed. In the disposable full-client wrapper, `cargo +nightly tree --target powerpc-nintendo-cafe -i libmimalloc-sys` printed no dependencies and a target check reached unsupported Winit, errno, if-addrs and rustls errors (log: `$scratch\horizonwiiu-client-cafe-allocator-check.log`). The Cafe allocator and census have not been linked or run; this is not a full client build or Cemu verification.
+- Uncommitted panorama, recipe-book and data-pack source extractions were set aside after review because they did not independently enable the Cafe client. A recoverable patch and the new files are in `$scratch\horizonwiiu-extraction-review-20261107`; `git apply --check` passed for its tracked patch. These experiments are not part of the project tree and should not be reapplied without demonstrating their necessity.
+- Keep Wii U compatibility behind opt-in target features and keep Horizon's desktop paths unchanged. The bootstrap selects Horizon-backed renderer diagnostic profiles: a reduced deterministic flat fixture or an Overworld generator without structures. The full Overworld path is host-verified, and Cemu has not yet completed Horizon's full block-model bake.
+- Do not try to run `wgpu` directly on GX2. `gx2gl` is an OpenGL 3.3 translation layer, not a `wgpu` backend; using it would still require a renderer and shader/resource adaptation.
+- Keep the CafeOS entry point in WUT C and Horizon systems in Rust. The normal `build.sh` path defaults to Horizon's Cafe-std generator, assets and canonical mesher, adapting region storage through borrowed `MeshChunk` views before sending vertices to GX2. This remains a renderer diagnostic: its free-fly camera has no player collision, interactions, gameplay loop or save path. Horizon's `Game` and save systems remain the intended sources of truth when platform APIs are connected. The non-vanilla hand-built chunk is now explicitly opt-in with `HORIZON_WIIU_DIAGNOSTIC_FIXTURE=1`; it is not the default RPX. The reduced flat Horizon generator profile is also only a deterministic renderer diagnostic, not a vanilla Superflat/client world. The terrain loader omits emissive data because GX2 has no emissive draw pass. Region generation uses Cafe `std::thread`; GPU conversion/upload stays on the render thread. Capture builds use Horizon-captured meshes, camera, atlas and lightmap. No desktop `wgpu` code is linked; translucent meshes remain unsupported. GCC 16 emits a zero-size `.note.GNU-stack` marker that `elf2rpl` rejects, so packaging strips that non-runtime marker from a copy of the ELF first.
+- `horizon-terrain-mesh` owns Horizon's original CPU mesher and `SectionMesh`; the desktop renderer preserves upstream paths with re-exports, and the Wii U region adapter borrows its block/biome storage. `horizon-client-platform` owns scheduling, portable events/input, and the original Horizon logger. The shared `Game` event handlers now accept platform events, but startup, Winit's application lifecycle, and `Game`'s concrete WGPU renderer remain coupled. The `PresentationBackend` / `TerrainBackend` API is still a desktop-only forwarding seam; GX2 is not selectable by the full client yet. Keep the bootstrap renderer diagnostic-only.
+
+- A full Cafe `std` route exists upstream on the [`rust-wiiu/rust` `powerpc-nintendo-cafe` branch](https://github.com/rust-wiiu/rust/tree/powerpc-nintendo-cafe), with Cafe implementations for filesystem, threads, time, networking, random, and standard I/O. A prior compiler is already available at `$workspace/HorizonWiiU-toolchain/rust-wiiu-cafe/build/host/stage1/bin/rustc` (rustc 1.101.0-dev, `powerpc-nintendo-cafe` target and `std` present) and has produced earlier worldgen RPXs. Use it to validate current changes; rebuilding the pinned Rust/LLVM sources is unnecessary unless this compiler fails. An exploratory checkout remains at `$scratch\horizonwiiu-rust-cafe-sparse` and is not part of the build path.
+
+## Next steps
+
+The concrete GX2 interface, parallel work packages, and their acceptance criteria
+are recorded in [wiiu/GX2_INTEGRATION.md](wiiu/GX2_INTEGRATION.md).
+
+The earlier CIT loader/armor extraction has now been validated and committed. On WSL, with `CARGO_TARGET_DIR=$scratch/horizonwiiu-cit-work` and `TMPDIR=$scratch/horizonwiiu-cit-tmp`, these focused checks passed:
